@*
    What the assistants have been doing on the machines that have reported: how many
    sessions touched the window, how long they ran, and when anything last moved.
    "Reported" rather than "this machine" since session records began replicating —
    the breakdown below lists a row per machine, and it is not this one alone.

    The one part on this surface whose figures come off local disk rather than out of a
    provider's API, which is what makes it the one part the machine filter can drive.
    The repository filter, on the other hand, deliberately does not reach it: Claude
    records no repository against a session, so filtering by one would silently drop
    every Claude session from a part whose whole point is showing both assistants. That
    is said in the note rather than left for a reader to deduce from figures that refuse
    to move.

    Three honesty flags travel with the figures and each has its own sentence. A capped
    read makes every number a floor. A folder that could not be read means half the
    picture is missing, and which half is named. A session whose start was never
    recorded is counted as a session and contributes no time, because a zero-length
    session would be a fact nobody wrote down.

    The active time carries a fourth caveat that is not a flag at all, and so is stated
    permanently rather than conditionally: overlapping sessions are summed rather than
    merged, and an hour in which three agents ran reads as three hours. Nothing on this
    surface can tell whether that happened, which is exactly why the sentence cannot wait
    for it.

    The chart counts a session into the ISO week it last moved, because that is the
    only instant every session has: the start is optional and no end is recorded at
    all. So a session that ran across a week boundary is one column's worth of work in
    the later week rather than half in each, and the caption says so — a reader who
    added the columns up expecting the weeks to divide the work between them would be
    reading a chart that never claimed to. A capped read makes the columns a floor
    exactly as it makes the tiles one, and the note says that too.

    The breakdown appears only when there is more than one row, on the Rework part's
    precedent: a one-row table of the thing already named in the filter is a
    restatement, not a comparison.
*@
@inherits DashboardPartBase<AssistantSessionsInsight>
@inject ISessionInsights Insights

<PartFrame Title="Assistant sessions"
           Note="@Note"
           Busy="Status == MetricStatusKind.Loading"
           OnRefresh="RefreshAsync"
           TestId="dashboard-sessions">
    @if (IsReady)
    {
        <div class="dashboard-part__stack">
            <MetricGrid Label="Assistant sessions over the selected window" TestId="dashboard-sessions-tiles">
                <MetricTile Label="Sessions"
                            Value="@MetricFormat.Compact(Value!.Sessions)"
                            Emphasis="true"
                            TestId="dashboard-sessions-count" />
                <MetricTile Label="Active time"
                            Value="@DashboardFormat.Duration(Value!.ActiveTime)"
                            Footnote="@ActiveTimeFootnote"
                            TestId="dashboard-sessions-active" />
                @* The instant rather than "two hours ago". A relative figure would need
                   a clock in a component that otherwise reads none, and would go stale
                   on screen while the surface sat open. *@
                <MetricTile Label="Last activity"
                            Value="@DashboardFormat.Moment(Value!.LastActivityAt)"
                            Footnote="@(Value!.LastActivityAt is null ? null : "UTC")"
                            TestId="dashboard-sessions-last" />
            </MetricGrid>

            @* Drawn only when there is an axis to draw it on. The part's empty state
               is governed by the session count, so a series that arrived empty for any
               other reason must not put "no usage in this period" under tiles that are
               reporting some. *@
            @if (Value!.SessionsPerWeek.Count > 0)
            {
                <MetricBars Points="@PerWeek"
                            Label="@PerWeekLabel"
                            BucketHeading="Week"
                            ValueHeading="Sessions"
                            HighlightLast="true"
                            FormatValue="MetricFormat.Compact"
                            TestId="dashboard-sessions-bars" />
            }

            @if (Value!.Breakdown.Count > 1)
            {
                <DataTable TItem="AssistantSessionRow"
                           Columns="@Columns"
                           Items="@Value!.Breakdown"
                           RowKey="@(row => row.Key)"
                           AriaLabel="@BreakdownLabel"
                           TestId="dashboard-sessions-breakdown">
                    <Row Context="row">
                        <td>@row.Name</td>
                        <td>@MetricFormat.Compact(row.Sessions)</td>
                        <td>@DashboardFormat.Duration(row.ActiveTime)</td>
                        <td>@DashboardFormat.Moment(row.LastActivityAt)</td>
                    </Row>
                </DataTable>
            }
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <MetricStatus Kind="Status" Message="@StatusMessage" TestId="dashboard-sessions-status" />
    }
</PartFrame>

@code {
    /// <summary>Claude records no repository against a session, so narrowing this part
    /// by one would drop every Claude session rather than filter it. The part refuses
    /// the dimension and says so on screen.</summary>
    protected override bool FollowsRepository => false;

    /// <summary>The one part on the surface that knows which machine its figures came
    /// from, so the one part the machine filter drives.</summary>
    protected override bool FollowsMachine => true;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the figures cover, and every way in which they are less than the whole
    /// truth. Assembled rather than written as one sentence because each clause is
    /// conditional and a note that claimed a floor was a total is the failure worth
    /// avoiding.
    /// </summary>
    private string Note
    {
        get
        {
            // "on this machine" was true until session records began replicating, and
            // the machine breakdown directly under this note has listed several ever
            // since — a note naming one machine above a table naming three is the kind
            // of stale sentence a reader trusts and should not.
            var basis = $"Read from what Claude and Copilot leave on the machines that have reported, over {Scope.Weeks} weeks. "
                + "Claude does not record a repository, so the repository filter does not change this part.";

            if (!IsReady) return basis;

            if (Value!.Capped)
            {
                basis += $" Only the newest {Value!.CapPerAssistant} sessions per assistant were read, "
                    + "so these figures are a floor, the weekly columns included.";
            }

            foreach (var source in Value!.Unreadable)
            {
                basis += $" {source}'s folder could not be read.";
            }

            return basis;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The weekly series as the chart component wants it. Names only, no arithmetic:
    /// the bucketing happened where the sessions were, and a second pass here would be
    /// a second place for it to drift.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MetricPoint> PerWeek =>
        IsReady
            ? [.. Value!.SessionsPerWeek.Select(point => new MetricPoint(point.Label, point.Value))]
            : [];

    /// <summary>
    /// What a column is, said where the columns are. A session has no end recorded and
    /// may have no start, so the week it last moved is the only week it can be counted
    /// in — and the consequence, that a session running across a boundary appears once
    /// rather than in both weeks, is the reader's to know rather than to discover from
    /// a chart whose columns do not sum to the tile above them.
    /// </summary>
    private const string PerWeekLabel =
        "Sessions per week, counted in the week they last moved — one that ran across two weeks appears once";

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DataTableColumn> MachineColumns =
    [
        new("Machine"),
        new("Sessions"),
        new("Active time"),
        new("Last activity")
    ];

    private static readonly IReadOnlyList<DataTableColumn> AssistantColumns =
    [
        new("Assistant"),
        new("Sessions"),
        new("Active time"),
        new("Last activity")
    ];

    /// <summary>The first column is named after whatever the breakdown is of, which is
    /// whichever question the filter has not already answered.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<DataTableColumn> Columns => Scope.IsAllMachines ? MachineColumns : AssistantColumns;

    private string BreakdownLabel => Scope.IsAllMachines ? "Sessions by machine" : "Sessions by assistant";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the active time is not. Two sentences, and only the first of them is always
    /// there.
    /// <para>
    /// The overlap sentence is permanent because the figure is permanently capable of
    /// exceeding the window it sits under: sessions are summed rather than merged — two
    /// agents running at once is two sessions' worth of work — so an hour of wall clock
    /// can read as three. A reader who takes that for elapsed time has been misled by a
    /// number nobody qualified, and the qualification cannot be conditional because the
    /// condition is invisible from here: this part never learns whether any two of its
    /// sessions overlapped.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The second is conditional for the opposite reason. "Excludes 0 sessions" on every
    /// complete reading would train the reader to stop reading footnotes, and then the
    /// first sentence would go unread too.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string ActiveTimeFootnote =>
        Concurrency + (Unstarted is { } unstarted ? $" {unstarted}" : string.Empty);

    private const string Concurrency =
        "Concurrent sessions are counted separately, so this can exceed elapsed time.";

    private string? Unstarted => IsReady && Value!.WithoutStart > 0
        ? $"Excludes {Value!.WithoutStart} {(Value!.WithoutStart == 1 ? "session" : "sessions")} "
            + "whose start was not recorded."
        : null;

    protected override Task<InsightResult<AssistantSessionsInsight>> FetchAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
        Insights.GetSessionsAsync(Scope, cancellationToken);

    /// <summary>
    /// Empty when nothing touched the window. Not "when the active time is zero": a
    /// window full of sessions none of which recorded a start is a real answer about
    /// how much has been going on, and hiding it behind an empty state would throw the
    /// count away along with the caveat.
    /// </summary>
    protected override bool IsEmpty(AssistantSessionsInsight value) => value.Sessions == 0;

    protected override string EmptyMessage => "No assistant session touched this window.";

    protected override void InvalidateSource() => Insights.Invalidate();
}
